==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|la|(s)tewp}}
From {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{m|ine-pro|*(s)tewp-}}. Cognates include {{etyl|grc|-}} {{m|grc|τύπτω||I strike}}, {{etyl|sa|-}} {{m|sa|sc=Deva|तोपति|tr=tópati||to hurt}}, {{etyl|sq|-}} {{m|sq|shtyj||to thrust}}, {{etyl|cu|-}} {{m|cu|тъпати|tr=tŭpati}}, and {{etyl|ang|-}} {{m|ang|styntan}} ({{etyl|en|-}} {{m|en|stint}}), Old English {{m|ang|stybb}} ({{etyl|en|-}} {{m|en|stub}}), Old English {{m|ang|stēap}} ({{etyl|en|-}} {{m|en|steep}})

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/ˈstu.pe.oː/|lang=la}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|stupeō|stupēre|stupuī|stupītum|conj=2|pattern=nopass}}

# {{label|la|intransitive}} I am [[stunned]], [[stiffened]] or [[benumbed]], [[stop]], [[hesitate]].
# {{label|la|intransitive}} I am [[dazed]], [[speechless]] or [[silenced]]; I am [[astounded]], [[confounded]], [[aghast]] or [[amazed]].
#* '''c. 50 {{C.E.}}''', [[w:Seneca the Younger|Seneca the Younger]], ''[[w:Phaedra (Seneca)|Phaedra]], 607
#*: Curae leues locuntur, ingentes '''stupent'''.
#*:: Trivial concerns talk, great ones are '''speechless'''.
# {{label|la|transitive}} I am [[astonished]] or [[amazed]] at, [[wonder]] at.

